_1812__coursoll_1808.glb\": \"c0d9006b495bc654bf2a29f5a6211db4cbaf6164d629a1682997642e6a8b079a\","},{"sha":"62ae8ab5090a4cf4c04cd7d865492348df1c2df9","filename":"chicago/4d/assets/web/coleman_ames_house_1811_prairie__as_mapped_1904.glb","status":"modified","additions":0,"deletions":0,"changes":0,"blob_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/blob/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fassets%2Fweb%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie__as_mapped_1904.glb","raw_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/raw/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fassets%2Fweb%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie__as_mapped_1904.glb","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kevinrhaas/chicago/contents/chicago%2F4d%2Fassets%2Fweb%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie__as_mapped_1904.glb?ref=e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854"},{"sha":"b11f693f7de8b83b1a9b072509a93e238b7aadac","filename":"chicago/4d/changelog.d/T-3025.json","status":"added","additions":7,"deletions":0,"changes":7,"blob_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/blob/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fchangelog.d%2FT-3025.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/raw/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fchangelog.d%2FT-3025.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kevinrhaas/chicago/contents/chicago%2F4d%2Fchangelog.d%2FT-3025.json?ref=e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@\n+{\n+  \"title\": \"Glessner's roofs ruled red tile, though every old print shows grey\",\n+  \"kind\": \"change\",\n+  \"items\": [\n+    \"Glessner House keeps its red, unglazed clay tile roofs, the stable cupola's included. Glessner's own account of the house names them, and the 1963 survey found clay tile under a later coat of tar. Old photographs show the roofs grey because they are black and white, and the 1960s ones show the tar.\"\n+  ]\n+}"},{"sha":"1ae67226bd3f27a82c667c5e780e52fe45dc5883","filename":"chicago/4d/changelog.d/T-3063.json","status":"added","additions":7,"deletions":0,"changes":7,"blob_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/blob/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fchangelog.d%2FT-3063.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/raw/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fchangelog.d%2FT-3063.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kevinrhaas/chicago/contents/chicago%2F4d%2Fchangelog.d%2FT-3063.json?ref=e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@\n+{\n+  \"title\": \"1811 Prairie's south wall now runs level behind its tall chimney\",\n+  \"kind\": \"improvement\",\n+  \"items\": [\n+    \"Behind the tall chimney, the top of 1811 Prairie's brick south wall no longer slopes down toward the back. It now runs level back to the end of the front block, as both old photographs show it, with the second chimney standing up from it.\"\n+  ]\n+}"},{"sha":"585d279ee8dd6e6cd9bcad8a022b882935ed7be3","filename":"chicago/4d/data/liberties.json","status":"modified","additions":38,"deletions":1,"changes":39,"blob_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/blob/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Fliberties.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/raw/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Fliberties.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kevinrhaas/chicago/contents/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Fliberties.json?ref=e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854","patch":"@@ -3,7 +3,7 @@\n   \"source\": \"docs/LIBERTIES.md\",\n   \"standard\": \"A visitor should be able to tell you which parts we made up.\",\n   \"note\": \"The confidence model covers attributes. These are the decisions that live above any single attribute: scope, omission, simplification, and the choices a visitor would otherwise have to reverse-engineer.\",\n-  \"count\": 702,\n+  \"count\": 703,\n   \"names\": [\n     {\n       \"id\": \"agency_striker_log_house\",\n@@ -51524,6 +51524,43 @@\n         \"T-3061\"\n       ]\n     },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"L-k01-1811-rear-fall-3063\",\n+      \"title\": \"1811 Prairie's roof behind the south gable falls steeply to a flat deck under a level coped wall head\",\n+      \"section\": \"per_subject\",\n+      \"subjects\": [\n+        \"coleman_ames_house_1811_prairie\"\n+      ],\n+      \"covers\": [],\n+      \"recorded\": \"2026-10-23\",\n+      \"revised\": null,\n+      \"fields\": [\n+        {\n+          \"label\": \"Decision\",\n+          \"text\": \"behind the tall stack, 1811 Prairie's south wall (K01's `north`) is built as a coped parapet whose coping's top runs LEVEL at 10.95 m back to the front block's rear wall, and the roof under it falls from the ridge at the street's 61.2 degrees to a flat deck 0.38 m under the coping, where earlier work had a plain rear slope falling at 22.4 degrees to the rear wall's eave and the coping following it down. The kit learns it (gable roof rear fall, coped ends only); every other K01 house re-emits byte-identical. **What is read, not invented.** Through the camera on the 1888 Inland Architect plate (\\\"Residence of Miner T. Ames, Chicago): the coping's top edge just clear of the tall stack at (528, 374-380) px and at the block's rear wall at (572, 485) px, which the camera puts on the wall's plane at u 5.9, 10.96 m and u -0.3, 10.98 m, a line running into the flank's vanishing point; the second stack's top 26-35 px over it. The Chicagology print (`cgn-1811-coleman-ames-photo`, rights unknown, read in the image store's display copy and not committed) draws the same from further south-west: a short steep fall from the tall stack, then one long coping line back past the second stack. Neither picture shows any roof over that coping. **What is invented.** What lies behind the coping: the fall's pitch (the street's own 61.2 degrees, the plainest choice; the plate's coping drops in about one column just behind the stack, which a fall nearer 75 degrees would match better), the deck (flat, falling a quarter inch to the foot to the rear wall, laid in the roof's slate where a tin deck is likelier; no picture sees it), and the parapet's head running on 0.1 m behind the ridge. The rendered fall spreads over x 528-533 px where the plate's drops at x 527-528. **Not changed here.** The plate and the print both step the flank down at the block's rear wall to a lower wall head of about 8.6 m over the brick range; the build carries the range at 11.4 m.\"\n+        },\n+        {\n+          \"label\": \"How to resolve\",\n+          \"text\": \"a view of the roof from above or behind (an aerial, a section, the 1965 HABS photograph that is to find), or earlier work's solved camera on the Chicagology print.\"\n+        },\n+        {\n+          \"label\": \"Review\",\n+          \"text\": \"the inland architect plate match data.\"\n+        },\n+        {\n+          \"label\": \"Recorded\",\n+          \"text\": \"2026-10-23 (T-3063).\"\n+        }\n+      ],\n+      \"work_refs\": [\n+        \"T-3077\",\n+        \"T-3050\",\n+        \"T-2541\",\n+        \"T-3052\",\n+        \"T-3063\",\n+        \"T-3051\"\n+      ]\n+    },\n     {\n       \"id\": \"L46\",\n       \"title\": \"The fort stands on a bank the model has no cut or fill for\","},{"sha":"31804a13a97c8cb4af9cbd5ff995045197abc746","filename":"chicago/4d/data/reference_views/coleman_ames_house_1811_prairie/inland_architect_plate.match.json","status":"modified","additions":3,"deletions":3,"changes":6,"blob_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/blob/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie%2Finland_architect_plate.match.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/raw/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie%2Finland_architect_plate.match.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kevinrhaas/chicago/contents/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie%2Finland_architect_plate.match.json?ref=e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854","patch":"@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n   \"structure_id\": \"coleman_ames_house_1811_prairie\",\n   \"view\": \"inland_architect_plate\",\n   \"glb\": \"assets/gltf/coleman_ames_house_1811_prairie__as_mapped_1904.glb\",\n-  \"glb_sha256\": \"66f0cabd3411b4cb563d27011a24544fce7fbcaa70225ec07ff80170ec383420\",\n+  \"glb_sha256\": \"2e70ce892dadfd60c5363553b727f3d8d9980b78058b456b4183ebd5667cf0bb\",\n   \"camera_sha256\": \"8f67a67f5a407ad30522085a1ab67f9c29ddc0bae6ceb1a979db9ea1accdc2b4\",\n   \"photo_sha256\": \"e55aede801c2193a857746efb4545fe97c995e697344b7e68ead1f06a4b7c7fe\",\n   \"side_by_side\": \"docs/RESEARCH/photo-match/coleman_ames_house_1811_prairie/inland_architect_plate-side-by-side.jpg\",\n@@ -149,8 +149,8 @@\n     },\n     {\n       \"what\": \"the south gable's rear fall: behind the tall stack the plate's coping runs from about (528, 380) to (557, 453) px, about 30 px under the render's (522, 322) to (555, 417), parallel to it, so the rear fall stands lower than the coped rake built at the ridge + 0.3 m falling to the rear wall head; the second stack stands on that lower line in the plate. Read the flank's wall head and the rear slope against the Chicagology print (T-3063)\",\n-      \"status\": \"open\",\n-      \"how\": \"\"\n+      \"status\": \"fixed\",\n+      \"how\": \"T-3063: K01's gable roof learns a rear fall (gable_roof.rear_fall): the rear slope falls from the ridge at the street's 61.2 degrees to a flat deck, and the coped parapet over it falls with it to a level wall head, its coping's top at 10.95 m, run back to the block's rear wall. Rendered, the coping's skyline stands at y 389-392 px at x 533-534 (the plate's 400-402) and y 461-471 at x 562-566 (the plate's 465-472), a level line into the flank's vanishing point; the second stack's top at y 394 from x 535 (the plate's 393-396 from x 535). Still differs: the fall itself spreads over x 528-533 where the plate drops in one column at x 527-528 (the render 11-33 px over the plate at x 529-532), as a 61.2-degree fall would; and beyond x 569 the render's range stands at its 11.4 m wall head (y 462-471) where the plate steps down at x 573 to about y 552, a lower wall head of about 8.6 m behind the front block (T-3077).\"\n     },\n     {\n       \"what\": \"colour: a monochrome print. The brown sandstone is the T-1837 register's reading\","},{"sha":"a3337edd2be73c11eae4d72e7aef9bcc672265ca","filename":"chicago/4d/data/reference_views/glessner_house/cornell-northwest-west-windows.match.json","status":"modified","additions":5,"deletions":1,"changes":6,"blob_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/blob/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fglessner_house%2Fcornell-northwest-west-windows.match.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/raw/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fglessner_house%2Fcornell-northwest-west-windows.match.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kevinrhaas/chicago/contents/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fglessner_house%2Fcornell-northwest-west-windows.match.json?ref=e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854","patch":"@@ -21,9 +21,13 @@\n       \"status\": \"fixed\"\n     },\n     {\n-      \"what\": \"West/north gable, cupola, portal and framing residuals remain. The west-plane camera uses retained reconstructed eaves, so height/camera uncertainty is not mistaken for independently surveyed geometry. Still open after T-3030 (the cupola's dark corner strips are fixed, T-3036): the pyramid's covering reads slate against the model's red tile (T-3025). The cupola's coursing, read by T-3044, is T-3046's (the fixed entry below).\",\n+      \"what\": \"West/north gable, cupola, portal and framing residuals remain. The west-plane camera uses retained reconstructed eaves, so height/camera uncertainty is not mistaken for independently surveyed geometry. Still open after T-3030 (the cupola's dark corner strips are fixed, T-3036): the cupola's coursing, read by T-3044, is T-3046's (the fixed entry below).\",\n       \"status\": \"open\"\n     },\n+    {\n+      \"what\": \"T-3025, ruled: the roofs and the cupola's pyramid read slate-grey in this c. 1887 print, and the record's red tile reads the same slate-grey when the render through this camera is reduced to luminance (docs/RESEARCH/glessner-roof-covering-T-3025/stable-roof-cornell.jpg). A red surface records dark on a dry plate of the period. The covering was red baked, unglazed terracotta tile (Glessner 1923, HABS data p. 13; HABS's 1963 survey finds terracotta, which excludes slate), and the record lays that. Colour in a monochrome print is not shown; nothing is rebaked. The print's course, about 6.4 in against the model's 5 in, is a module question filed separately (README § 3).\",\n+      \"status\": \"not shown\"\n+    },\n     {\n       \"what\": \"T-3046 cupola coursing: the stone body is laid in rock-faced courses where the render had smooth plates, as T-3044 read this print. The shaft under the lower pigeon ledge is four courses, of which the part clear of the ridge shows two or three bed joints here; each pigeon-hole tier is one course between its ledges, and the band over the top tier one. The print does not resolve the vertical joints, so block lengths follow the house's own rule (docs/LIBERTIES.md L-glessner-cupola-coursing-3046).\",\n       \"status\": \"fixed\""},{"sha":"c7093b373afe93317f45fe6f8e338858b6e7945e","filename":"chicago/4d/data/reference_views/glessner_house/habs-01-northwest.match.json","status":"modified","additions":5,"deletions":1,"changes":6,"blob_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/blob/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fglessner_house%2Fhabs-01-northwest.match.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/raw/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fglessner_house%2Fhabs-01-northwest.match.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kevinrhaas/chicago/contents/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fglessner_house%2Fhabs-01-northwest.match.json?ref=e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854","patch":"@@ -16,9 +16,13 @@\n       \"status\": \"fixed\"\n     },\n     {\n-      \"what\": \"T-2849, reviewed from the rebaked master: the cupola body is now granite as the print shows its coursed shaft below the band (it was boarding), on stone landing ledges 0.25 ft deep; the gable's ledge now carries the two corbels this print shows under it, with the 5-3-1 pigeon holes at the spacing HABS photograph 15 measures. Still open after T-3030: the pyramid's covering reads slate, not the model's red tile (T-3025); the cupola's corners carry a dark vertical strip from the band to the roof that neither northwest print shows (T-3036); the stable end sits about 28 px off because this camera is fitted at the Prairie corner (T-2758).\",\n+      \"what\": \"T-2849, reviewed from the rebaked master: the cupola body is now granite as the print shows its coursed shaft below the band (it was boarding), on stone landing ledges 0.25 ft deep; the gable's ledge now carries the two corbels this print shows under it, with the 5-3-1 pigeon holes at the spacing HABS photograph 15 measures. Still open after T-3030: the cupola's corners carry a dark vertical strip from the band to the roof that neither northwest print shows (T-3036); the stable end sits about 28 px off because this camera is fitted at the Prairie corner (T-2758).\",\n       \"status\": \"open\"\n     },\n+    {\n+      \"what\": \"T-3025, ruled: the covering this print reads slate-grey, the cupola's pyramid included, is the 1963 tar HABS records over the tiles (data p. 21), not the 1904 surface. The roofs were red baked, unglazed terracotta tile (Glessner 1923, HABS data p. 13; HABS's 1963 survey finds terracotta under the tar, which excludes slate), and the record lays that. Colour in a monochrome print is not shown; nothing is rebaked. docs/RESEARCH/glessner-roof-covering-T-3025/README.md.\",\n+      \"status\": \"not shown\"\n+    },\n     {\n       \"what\": \"T-2228: the cupola centre reads about 22 px (roughly 4 ft by parallax) north of the owner-directed S14.6, kept; the whole stable roof reads about 28 px low in this camera, which is camera/height uncertainty, not yet a roof correction. T-2758: it is the camera, not the roof. This camera's only vertical controls are the grade and eave at the Prairie corner (W 0), 141 ft east of the stable, so at the stable it draws 14.6 px a foot where the carriage doorway (sheet 2 jambs W 134.9-147.0, sheet 5 soffit 12.6 ft) measures 13.85 in the print. Re-solved with the doorway's four corners added (eye height or image lean free in turn), it reads the gable's coping apex at 38.1-38.6 ft and the cupola's apex at 45.5-46.3 ft on the model's centre (model 39.6 and 47.0), and the doorway as a bare ruler reads the coping apex at 38.8 ft. The residual of about 1 ft is inside the ridge's 1.5 ft tolerance and is no larger than the Cornell view's re-solved disagreement with it (38.3-39.5 ft), so the roof is not changed. The 4 ft parallax was not a reading: every control in this photograph lies in the north face, so its depth is free: with the pixel aspect let go, which those controls cannot measure, fits of one RMS (3.9 px) slide the cupola from S14.4 at square pixels to S10.4 at an aspect of 1.06 while the gable's height does not move. The Cornell view, re-solved on two faces, puts it at S13.0-13.2, about 1.5 ft north of S14.6, which does not hold up as a question for the owner. The doorway re-solve is not adopted here: it moves the north tower's finial about 9 px off the print where this camera holds it to 1.5 px, so one level pinhole does not fit both ends of this print. data/comparisons/glessner/stable-roof-heights.json (tools/glessner_stable_roof_read.py).\",\n       \"status\": \"fixed\""},{"sha":"3d7a6791614c4d7f7a78a45b014acfd4cf7dc6b6","filename":"chicago/4d/data/reference_views/glessner_house/habs-15-stable-detail.match.json","status":"modified","additions":5,"deletions":1,"changes":6,"blob_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/blob/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fglessner_house%2Fhabs-15-stable-detail.match.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/raw/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fglessner_house%2Fhabs-15-stable-detail.match.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kevinrhaas/chicago/contents/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Freference_views%2Fglessner_house%2Fhabs-15-stable-detail.match.json?ref=e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854","patch":"@@ -12,9 +12,13 @@\n       \"status\": \"fixed\"\n     },\n     {\n-      \"what\": \"Still open at the stable end: the print hides the gable apex and the cupola behind its film holder, so neither is judged from this view. The roof covering reads slate-grey against the model's red tile (house-wide, T-3025). The camera's roll (about 1.2 degrees) and its 13-20 px offset at the loft still shift the hoist about 15 px west of the print's.\",\n+      \"what\": \"Still open at the stable end: the print hides the gable apex and the cupola behind its film holder, so neither is judged from this view. The camera's roll (about 1.2 degrees) and its 13-20 px offset at the loft still shift the hoist about 15 px west of the print's.\",\n       \"status\": \"open\"\n     },\n+    {\n+      \"what\": \"T-3025, ruled: the roof covering reads slate-grey here because this 1965 print shows the tar HABS recorded over the tiles in 1963 (data p. 21). The roofs were red baked, unglazed terracotta tile, house-wide (Glessner 1923, HABS data p. 13; the 1963 survey finds terracotta under the tar, which excludes slate), and the record lays that. Colour in a monochrome print is not shown; nothing is rebaked. docs/RESEARCH/glessner-roof-covering-T-3025/README.md.\",\n+      \"status\": \"not shown\"\n+    },\n     {\n       \"what\": \"The overhead garage leaf and closed loft opening are later alterations; original1904 opening treatments are tested against earlier evidence, not copied from this1965 plate. Cars/signs/street fabric are outside this house-only comparison.\",\n       \"status\": \"not shown\""},{"sha":"c0ee2ec1cbecfd939348bf4ab278141267aac162","filename":"chicago/4d/data/sidecars/1904/coleman_ames_house_1811_prairie.json","status":"modified","additions":6,"deletions":1,"changes":7,"blob_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/blob/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Fsidecars%2F1904%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/raw/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Fsidecars%2F1904%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kevinrhaas/chicago/contents/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Fsidecars%2F1904%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie.json?ref=e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854","patch":"@@ -747,6 +747,11 @@\n           \"apex_v_m\": 3.3,\n           \"attic_from_m\": 5.0\n         },\n+        \"rear_fall\": {\n+          \"wall_head_m\": 10.95,\n+          \"pitch_deg\": 61.2,\n+          \"head_back_m\": 0.1\n+        },\n         \"ends\": {\n           \"north\": {\n             \"style\": \"coped\"\n@@ -757,7 +762,7 @@\n       \"sources\": [\n         \"inland_architect_1888_10_ames_residence\"\n       ],\n-      \"note\": \"T-3050, read through the T-2541 camera on the 1888 plate and checked on the Chicagology print (cgn-1811-coleman-ames-photo, a second negative from nearly the same place, which shows the south flank whole). The roof is two masses, not one ridge. Over the bay (K01's low-v end) a steep HIPPED mass, its apex at the plate's x 313-329 px, y 243-247, which the camera puts at u 3.8, v 3.8-4.2 and 16.0 m: a K05 hip over the whole depth from v 0 to 8.0 m at the street's 61.2 degrees reaches 16.01 m there, its south hip arris crossing y 335 at x 386 (the plate's 390). South of it a LOWER gable roof in the same street plane, its ridge 1.4 m behind the front at 11.68 m: the plate's level slate top at y 345-352 between the oriel's pyramid and the stack (x 448-498) lies on that line, and the south wall's coped gable rises from the front corner at the street's pitch to the tall stack and falls behind it, as both pictures draw it; a ridge at half the depth (T-2542's 3.78 m) would stand the coped rake in the sky the plate shows at x 541, y 335. Only the south (K01 'north') wall is carried up as a coped parapet; the north wall is under the hip's end. The ridge's depth is inferred to about +-0.4 m (the camera's depth along the flank is good to about +-1 m); the rear slope falls at 22.6 degrees to the wall head, its fall unseen.\"\n+      \"note\": \"T-3050, read through the T-2541 camera on the 1888 plate and checked on the Chicagology print (cgn-1811-coleman-ames-photo, a second negative from nearly the same place, which shows the south flank whole). The roof is two masses, not one ridge. Over the bay (K01's low-v end) a steep HIPPED mass, its apex at the plate's x 313-329 px, y 243-247, which the camera puts at u 3.8, v 3.8-4.2 and 16.0 m: a K05 hip over the whole depth from v 0 to 8.0 m at the street's 61.2 degrees reaches 16.01 m there, its south hip arris crossing y 335 at x 386 (the plate's 390). South of it a LOWER gable roof in the same street plane, its ridge 1.4 m behind the front at 11.68 m: the plate's level slate top at y 345-352 between the oriel's pyramid and the stack (x 448-498) lies on that line, and the south wall's coped gable rises from the front corner at the street's pitch to the tall stack and falls behind it, as both pictures draw it; a ridge at half the depth (T-2542's 3.78 m) would stand the coped rake in the sky the plate shows at x 541, y 335. Only the south (K01 'north') wall is carried up as a coped parapet; the north wall is under the hip's end. The ridge's depth is inferred to about +-0.4 m (the camera's depth along the flank is good to about +-1 m); the rear slope falls at 22.6 degrees to the wall head, its fall unseen. T-3063, the rear fall, read through the same camera on the plate and checked on the Chicagology print: behind the tall stack the south wall's coping runs LEVEL, not down a rear slope. On the plate its top edge stands at (528, 374-380) px just clear of the stack and at (572, 485) px at the block's rear wall, which the camera puts on the wall's plane (v 10.4) at u 5.9, 10.96 m and u -0.3, 10.98 m, a line running into the flank's vanishing point; the second stack stands on it, 26-35 px over it. The print, from further south-west, draws the same: a short steep fall from the tall stack, then a long coping line back past the second stack to a step down at the block's rear. Neither picture shows any roof over that coping, where the plain 22.4-degree rear slope stood the coping 0.5-1.4 m higher and its own covering over it near the ridge. So `rear_fall`: the coping's top level at 10.95 m from the rear wall to where the fall meets the deck; the parapet's head runs on only 0.1 m behind the ridge (the plate's coping has already fallen at u 5.9, just behind the stack), and the rear slope falls from the ridge at the street's own 61.2 degrees to a flat deck 0.38 m under the coping (K05's 0.3 m upstand and the 0.08 m coping) running back to the rear wall. The head's level height (10.95 m) is read; the fall's pitch and the deck are inferred (no picture sees behind the coping), the street's pitch the plainest choice; L-k01-1811-rear-fall-3063.\"\n     },\n     \"dormer\": {\n       \"value\": {"},{"sha":"5cfcda4e956be81470302730642dd2efe25a2729","filename":"chicago/4d/data/structures/coleman_ames_house_1811_prairie.json","status":"modified","additions":6,"deletions":1,"changes":7,"blob_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/blob/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Fstructures%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie.json","raw_url":"https://github.com/kevinrhaas/chicago/raw/e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Fstructures%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie.json","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kevinrhaas/chicago/contents/chicago%2F4d%2Fdata%2Fstructures%2Fcoleman_ames_house_1811_prairie.json?ref=e3baf9323b06a1fb66d3a506b7430f9f10cd7854","patch":"@@ -753,6 +753,11 @@\n               \"apex_v_m\": 3.3,\n               \"attic_from_m\": 5.0\n             },\n+            \"rear_fall\": {\n+              \"wall_head_m\": 10.95,\n+              \"pitch_deg\": 61.2,\n+              \"head_back_m\": 0.1\n+            },\n             \"ends\": {\n               \"north\": {\n                 \"style\": \"coped\"\n@@ -763,7 +768,7 @@\n           \"sources\": [\n             \"inland_architect_1888_10_ames_residence\"\n           ],\n-          \"note\": \"T-3050, read through the T-2541 camera on the 1888 plate and checked on the Chicagology print (cgn-1811-coleman-ames-photo, a second negative from nearly the same place, which shows the south flank whole). The roof is two masses, not one ridge. Over the bay (K01's low-v end) a steep HIPPED mass, its apex at the plate's x 313-329 px, y 243-247, which the camera puts at u 3.8, v 3.8-4.2 and 16.0 m: a K05 hip over the whole depth from v 0 to 8.0 m at the street's 61.2 degrees reaches 16.01 m there, its south hip arris crossing y 335 at x 386 (the plate's 390). South of it a LOWER gable roof in the same street plane, its ridge 1.4 m behind the front at 11.68 m: the plate's level slate top at y 345-352 between the oriel's pyramid and the stack (x 448-498) lies on that line, and the south wall's coped gable rises from the front corner at the street's pitch to the tall stack and